why did you change animation to none\
That line only applies inside 
\f3\fs26 \strokec3 @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)
\f1\fs24 \strokec3  \'97 it doesn't turn the animation off for everyone. It only kicks in for people who have a system-level setting turned on (in macOS: System Settings \uc0\u8594  Accessibility \u8594  Display \u8594  Reduce Motion; similar options exist on Windows, iOS, Android).\
Some people enable that setting because large moving backgrounds can cause dizziness, nausea, or headaches \'97 it's a real accessibility need, not just a preference. Websites are expected to respect it by turning off decorative animations like this pan/zoom effect when that flag is on.\
For the vast majority of visitors (anyone without that setting enabled), the banner will still animate exactly as designed \'97 slow zoom and pan, no change. The 
\f3\fs26 \strokec3 animation:none
\f1\fs24 \strokec3  rule is a fallback that only a smaller group of visitors will ever actually trigger.\
If you'd rather skip this and have the animation always play regardless of that setting, you can just leave that line out \'97 it's optional, not required for the effect to work. But I'd lean toward keeping it in, since it's a low-cost way to make the site friendlier for people who need it.\
